{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "phase_shift",
      "parent_relation": "The parent maps (S,I) to dI_dt with a time-invariant recovery loss recovery_rate*I, so for fixed transmission parameters the decline rate at a given I depends only on S through the infection term and cannot change systematically with time spent infected.",
      "child_relation": "Relax constant per-capita recovery by allowing recovery pressure to evolve during the outbreak through one additional latent disease-phase state, so dI_dt depends on both current prevalence and the current occupancy of a slower or faster recovery phase rather than only on total I.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "S",
        "I",
        "recovery_rate"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "During outbreaks with the same initial conditions and fixed transmission conditions, the child can produce a sharper or delayed turnover and a decline rate at matched I that differs between the rising and falling sides of the epidemic because infectious individuals are not all equally recoverable at every moment."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "force_of_infection_sat",
        "expression": "transmission_rate*S*I/(1 + saturation_strength*I)",
        "role": "Current saturated influx into the infectious class"
      },
      {
        "name": "net_growth_parent",
        "expression": "transmission_rate*S/(1 + saturation_strength*I) - recovery_rate",
        "role": "Parent per-capita infectious growth rate determining rise versus decline"
      },
      {
        "name": "susceptible_depletion",
        "expression": "1 - S",
        "role": "Cumulative epidemic progress in this closed population"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when the infectious-period distribution is not approximately exponential, so the outbreak timescale is comparable to the mean infectious duration and phase structure becomes appreciable across the rise, peak, and decline under the given initial condition.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the same mechanism appears as asymmetric growth and decay around the peak at similar I values; in holdout it extends to shifted peak timing and altered post-peak steepness without changing the closed-population SIR setting.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit where the added infectious phase collapses to an exponential-memory description, making total infectious individuals recover with an effectively constant per-capita rate recovery_rate.",
    "freedom_change": "add_state",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "A non-exponential infectious-period effect cannot be represented with the existing total-I state alone because any model using only current S and I with fixed parameters remains memoryless in I; one additional state is needed to encode infectious-phase occupancy that feeds back on dI_dt.",
    "gap_statement": "The model treats all infectious individuals as having the same instantaneous recovery hazard, omitting disease-age or phase structure within the infectious class.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Recovery that removes infectious individuals at a constant per-capita rate regardless of time since becoming infectious.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Stage-structured infectiousness or symptom progression causing recovery/removal to depend on occupancy of an infectious subphase rather than uniformly on total current prevalence.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "I",
      "recovery_rate",
      "S"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the exponential infectious-period assumption. The parent target mapping is memoryless: with transmission_rate, recovery_rate, and saturation_strength fixed, dI_dt at any observed (S,I) is fully determined, so matched (S,I) points on the ascending and descending limbs must have the same instantaneous derivative. That is a single-phase shortcut. A stage-structured infectious class changes the target mapping itself, not merely auxiliary trajectories, because the loss term from I can depend on how many infectious individuals occupy an early versus late subphase. Varying epidemic time along the observed single outbreak while holding the transmission and saturation parameters fixed, the child can show a different decline steepness or turnover timing at the same approximate I due to latent phase occupancy accumulated from prior incidence. This contrast is admissible when the infectious-period distribution is meaningfully non-exponential over the outbreak window; if the distribution is close to exponential, the added state may be weakly identifiable and the parent becomes an adequate approximation. The scientific continuation path is the limit where phase structure collapses and total I again recovers with constant per-capita rate."
  },
